ect not possible for >2 points among these points. So we need to pair them.\n\nLet's list all possible pairs with allowed slopes (not 0, infinite, -1). We'll need to ensure each point appears exactly once.\n\nPoints list with coordinates (a,b). Let's denote them as:\n\nP1 = (n,1)\nP2 = (n-1,1)\nP3 = (n-1,2)\nP4 = (n-2,1)\nP5 = (n-2,2)\nP6 = (n-2,3)\nP7 = (n-3,1)\nP8 = (n-3,2)\nP9 = (n-3,3)\nP10 = (n-3,4)\n\nWe need to find 5 pairs (edges) such that each pair's slope is not 0, infinite, -1.\n\nCompute slopes for each possible pair (ij). Let's compute differences:\n\n- Pair (P1,P2): slope (1-1)/(n-1 - n) = 0/(-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed.\n- (P1,P3): slope (2-1)/(n-1 - n) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- (P1,P4): slope (1-1)/(n-2 - n) = 0/(-2) = 0, not allowed.\n- (P1,P5): slope (2-1)/(n-2 - n) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, allowed.\n- (P1,P6): slope (3-1)/(n-2 - n) = 2/(-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- (P1,P7): slope (1-1)/(n-3 - n) = 0/(-3) = 0, not allowed.\n- (P1,P8): slope (2-1)/(n-3 - n) = 1/(-3) = -1/3, allowed.\n- (P1,P9): slope (3-1)/(n-3 - n) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, allowed.\n- (P1,P10): slope (4-1)/(n-3 - n) = 3/(-3) = -1, not allowed.\n\n- (P2,P3): slope (2-1)/(n-1 - (n-1)) = 1/0 = infinite, vertical, not allowed.\n- (P2,P4): slope (1-1)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 0/(-1) = 0, not allowed.\n- (P2,P5): slope (2-1)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- (P2,P6): slope (3-1)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 2/(-1) = -2, allowed.\n- (P2,P7): slope (1-1)/(n-3 - (n-1)) = 0/(-2) = 0, not allowed.\n- (P2,P8): slope (2-1)/(n-3 - (n-1)) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, allowed.\n- (P2,P9): slope (3-1)/(n-3 - (n-1)) = 2/(-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- (P2,P10): slope (4-1)/(n-3 - (n-1)) = 3/(-2) = -1.5, allowed (slope -3/2). That's allowed.\n\n- (P3,P4): slope (1-2)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = -1/(-1) = 1, allowed.\n- (P3,P5): slope (2-2)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 0/(-1) = 0, not allowed.\n- (P3,P6): slope (3-2)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- (P3,P7): slope (1-2)/(n-3 - (n-1)) = -1/(-2) = 0.5, allowed.\n- (P3,P8): slope (2-2)/(n-3 - (n-1)) = 0/(-2) = 0, not allowed.\n- (P3,P9): slope (3-2)/(n-3 - (n-1)) = 1/(-2) = -0.5, allowed.\n- (P3,P10): slope (4-2)/(n-3 - (n-1)) = 2/(-2) = -1, not allowed.\n\n- (P4,P5): slope (2-1)/(n-2 - (n-2)) = 1/0 = infinite, not allowed.\n- (P4,P6): slope (3-1)/(n-2 - (n-2)) = 2/0 = infinite, not allowed.\n- (P4,P7): slope (1-1)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 0/(-1) = 0, not allowed.\n- (P4,P8): slope (2-1)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- (P4,P9): slope (3-1)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 2/(-1) = -2, allowed.\n- (P4,P10): slope (4-1)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 3/(-1) = -3, allowed.\n\n- (P5,P6): slope (3-2)/(n-2 - (n-2)) = 1/0 = infinite, not allowed.\n- (P5,P7): slope (1-2)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = -1/(-1) = 1, allowed.\n- (P5,P8): slope (2-2)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 0/(-1) = 0, not allowed.\n- (P5,P9): slope (3-2)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- (P5,P10): slope (4-2)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 2/(-1) = -2, allowed.\n\n- (P6,P7): slope (1-3)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = -2/(-1) = 2, allowed.\n- (P6,P8): slope (2-3)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = -1/(-1) = 1, allowed.\n- (P6,P9): slope (3-3)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 0/(-1) = 0, not allowed.\n- (P6,P10): slope (4-3)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed.\n\n- (P7,P8): slope (2-1)/(n-3 - (n-3)) = 1/0 = infinite, not allowed.\n- (P7,P9): slope (3-1)/(n-3 - (n-3)) = 2/0 = infinite, not allowed.\n- (P7,P10): slope (4-1)/(n-3 - (n-3)) = 3/0 = infinite, not allowed.\n\n- (P8,P9): slope (3-2)/(n-3 - (n-3)) = 1/0 = infinite, not allowed.\n- (P8,P10): slope (4-2)/(n-3 - (n-3)) = 2/0 = infinite, not allowed.\n\n- (P9,P10): slope (4-3)/(n-3 - (n-3)) = 1/0 = infinite, not allowed.\n\nThus, allowed pairs (with allowed slopes) are:\n\n- (P1,P5) slope -0.5\n- (P1,P8) slope -1/3\n- (P1,P9) slope -2/3\n- (P2,P6) slope -2\n- (P2,P8) slope -0.5\n- (P2,P10) slope -1.5\n- (P3,P4) slope 1\n- (P3,P7) slope 0.5\n- (P3,P9) slope -0.5\n- (P4,P9) slope -2\n- (P4,P10) slope -3\n- (P5,P7) slope 1\n- (P5,P10) slope -2\n- (P6,P7) slope 2\n- (P6,P8) slope 1\n\nAlso (P2,P6) slope -2, (P2,P8) slope -0.5, (P2,P10) slope -1.5, (P3,P4) slope 1, (P3,P7) slope 0.5, (P3,P9) slope -0.5, (P4,P9) slope -2, (P4,P10) slope -3, (P5,P7) slope 1, (P5,P10) slope -2, (P6,P7) slope 2, (P6,P8) slope 1.\n\nAlso (P1,P5) slope -0.5, (P1,P8) slope -1/3, (P1,P9) slope -2/3.\n\nThus, we have many possible allowed pairs.\n\nNow we need to find a perfect matching covering all 10 points using these allowed pairs. Let's try to find a matching.\n\nWe can treat this as a graph with vertices P1..P10 and edges as allowed pairs. We need a perfect matching.\n\nLet's try to find a matching manually.\n\nOne approach: Use the known k=3 construction pairs: (P1,P5) (P2,P6) (P3,P4). That's three pairs covering P1,P5,P2,P6,P3,P4. That leaves P7,P8,P9,P10. We need to pair them with allowed edges.\n\nRemaining vertices: P7,P8,P9,P10.\n\nAllowed edges among these: (P4,P9) and (P4,P10) involve P4 which is already used. (P5,P7) uses P5 used. (P5,P10) uses P5 used. (P6,P7) uses P6 used. (P6,P8) uses P6 used. (P2,P8) uses P2 used. (P2,P10) uses P2 used. (P3,P7) uses P3 used. (P3,P9) uses P3 used. (P1,P8) uses P1 used. (P1,P9) uses P1 used. So we cannot pair the remaining four points using only edges among them because there are no allowed edges among P7,P8,P9,P10 (all pairs among them are vertical/infinite). Indeed, any pair among P7,P8,P9,P10 are vertical (same a coordinate) => infinite slope, not allowed. So we cannot pair them directly. So we need to adjust the matching to involve cross edges between the remaining points and some of the already used points, but then those used points would be paired twice, which is not allowed. So we need a different matching that pairs each point exactly once, possibly pairing P7 with some point from earlier columns, and then that earlier point's partner must be something else, etc. So we need to find a perfect matching across all 10 vertices.\n\nLet's try to find a matching that pairs each of P7,P8,P9,P10 with some other point among P1..P6.\n\nWe have edges connecting P7 to P3 (slope 0.5), P5 (slope 1), P6 (slope 2). Also P7 to P2? No, (P2,P7) slope 0, not allowed. P7 to P1? (P1,P7) slope 0, not allowed. P7 to P4? (P4,P7) slope 0, not allowed. So P7 can be paired with P3, P5, P6.\n\nP8 can be paired with P1 (slope -1/3), P2 (slope -0.5), P6 (slope 1). Also P8 with P5? (P5,P8) slope 0, not allowed. P8 with P3? (P3,P8) slope 0, not allowed. P8 with P4? (P4,P8) slope -1, not allowed. P8 with P7? vertical, not allowed. P8 with P9? vertical, not allowed. P8 with P10? vertical, not allowed.\n\nP9 can be paired with P1 (slope -2/3), P3 (slope -0.5), P4 (slope -2). Also P9 with P2? (P2,P9) slope -1, not allowed. P9 with P5? (P5,P9) slope -1, not allowed. P9 with P6? vertical, not allowed. P9 with P7? vertical, not allowed. P9 with P8? vertical, not allowed. P9 with P10? vertical, not allowed.\n\nP10 can be paired with P2 (slope -1.5), P4 (slope -3), P5 (slope -2). Also P10 with P1? slope -1, not allowed. P10 with P3? slope -1, not allowed. P10 with P6? slope -1, not allowed. P10 with P7? vertical, not allowed. P10 with P8? vertical, not allowed. P10 with P9? vertical, not allowed.\n\nThus, we have a bipartite-like structure: P7 can pair with {P3,P5,P6}, P8 with {P1,P2,P6}, P9 with {P1,P3,P4}, P10 with {P2,P4,P5}.\n\nWe need to find a perfect matching covering all 10 vertices. This is a matching problem. Let's try to find a matching manually.\n\nWe need to match each of P1..P10 exactly once. Let's try to assign pairs:\n\n- Pair P7 with P5 (allowed slope 1). Then P5 is used.\n- Pair P8 with P2 (allowed slope -0.5). Then P2 is used.\n- Pair P9 with P4 (allowed slope -2). Then P4 is used.\n- Pair P10 with P? Options: P2 (used), P4 (used), P5 (used). So P10 cannot be paired now. So we need to adjust.\n\nAlternatively, pair P7 with P6 (slope 2). Then P6 used.\n- Pair P8 with P1 (slope -1/3). Then P1 used.\n- Pair P9 with P4 (slope -2). Then P4 used.\n- Pair P10 with P5 (slope -2). Then P5 used.\nNow we have used P1,P4,P5,P6,P7,P8,P9,P10. Remaining vertices: P2,P3. Are they allowed to pair? (P2,P3) slope infinite, not allowed. So this fails.\n\nTry different combination:\n\n- Pair P7 with P3 (slope 0.5). Then P3 used.\n- Pair P8 with P6 (slope 1). Then P6 used.\n- Pair P9 with P4 (slope -2). Then P4 used.\n- Pair P10 with P5 (slope -2). Then P5 used.\nNow used: P3,P4,P5,P6,P7,P8,P9,P10. Remaining: P1,P2. Pair (P1,P2) slope 0, not allowed. So fails.\n\nTry:\n\n- Pair P7 with P3 (0.5)\n- Pair P8 with P2 (-0.5)\n- Pair P9 with P1 (-2/3)\n- Pair P10 with P5 (-2)\nNow used: P1,P2,P3,P5,P7,P8,P9,P10. Remaining: P4,P6. Pair (P4,P6) slope infinite (vertical), not allowed. So fails.\n\nTry:\n\n- Pair P7 with P5 (1)\n- Pair P8 with P1 (-1/3)\n- Pair P9 with P3 (-0.5)\n- Pair P10 with P4 (-3)\nNow used: P1,P3,P4,P5,P7,P8,P9,P10. Remaining: P2,P6. Pair (P2,P6) slope -2, allowed! Great. So we have a perfect matching:\n\nPairs:\n1. (P7,P5) slope 1\n2. (P8,P1) slope -1/3\n3. (P9,P3) slope -0.5\n4. (P10,P4) slope -3\n5. (P2,P6) slope -2\n\nCheck each pair's slope is allowed (not 0, infinite, -1). Yes:\n\n- (P7,P5): slope (2-1)/(n-3 - (n-2)) = 1/(-1) = -1? Wait compute: P7 = (n-3,1), P5 = (n-2,2). Slope = (2-1)/(n-2 - (n-3)) = 1/1 = 1. Actually (n-2) - (n-3) = 1, so slope = 1, allowed.\n\n- (P8,P1): P8 = (n-3,2), P1 = (n,1). Slope = (1-2)/(n - (n-3)) = -1/3 = -1/3, allowed.\n\n- (P9,P3): P9 = (n-3,3), P3 = (n-1,2). Slope = (2-3)/((n-1)-(n-3)) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed.\n\n- (P10,P4): P10 = (n-3,4), P4 = (n-2,1). Slope = (1-4)/((n-2)-(n-3)) = -3/1 = -3, allowed.\n\n- (P2,P6): P2 = (n-1,1), P6 = (n-2,3). Slope = (3-1)/((n-2)-(n-1)) = 2/(-1) = -2, allowed.\n\nThus we have a perfect matching covering all 10 points with 5 sunny lines. So k=5 is possible! Let's verify that these lines are distinct and not parallel to axes or slope -1. They have slopes 1, -1/3, -0.5, -3, -2. All distinct slopes, none are 0, infinite, -1. So they are all sunny lines. So we can have k=5 sunny lines covering the rightmost columns. Then we would have n-5 vertical lines covering columns 1,...,n-5. So total lines = n. So k=5 works for n≥5. For n=3, we have only columns 1,2,3. Let's check if k=5 is possible for n=3? No, because n=3, we have only 3 lines total, cannot have 5 sunny lines. So k must be ≤ n. So for n≥5, k=5 is possible. But the problem asks for all nonnegative integers k such that there exist n distinct lines satisfying the conditions. The answer may depend on n. The problem statement: \"Let n≥3 be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers k such that there exist n distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following: ... exactly k of the lines are sunny.\" So for each n, we need to find all possible k values (depending on n). The answer may be a set that depends on n. The solution says \"admissible values are k ∈ {0,1,3}\". That suggests that for any n≥3, the only possible k are 0,1,3. But we just found a construction for k=5 for n≥5. Let's verify if the construction indeed works for all n≥5. Let's test for n=5: n=5, columns are a=1..5. Rightmost columns a=2,3,4,5 (since n-3=2). Points in columns:\n\n- a=5: (5,1)\n- a=4: (4,1),(4,2)\n- a=3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3)\n- a=2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4)\n\nOur matching pairs for n=5:\n\n- P7 = (n-3,1) = (2,1) paired with P5 = (n-2,2) = (3,2). That's line slope 1, passes through (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\n- P8 = (n-3,2) = (2,2) paired with P1 = (n,1) = (5,1). That's line slope -1/3, passes through (2,2) and (5,1). Check: slope = (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3, okay.\n\n- P9 = (n-3,3) = (2,3) paired with P3 = (n-1,2) = (4,2). That's line slope -0.5, passes through (2,3) and (4,2). Check: slope = (2-3)/(4-2) = -1/2, okay.\n\n- P10 = (n-3,4) = (2,4) paired with P4 = (n-2,1) = (3,1). That's line slope -3, passes through (2,4) and (3,1). Check: slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, okay.\n\n- P2 = (n-1,1) = (4,1) paired with P6 = (n-2,3) = (3,3). That's line slope -2, passes through (4,1) and (3,3). Check: slope = (3-1)/(3-4) = 2/(-1) = -2, okay.\n\nThus we have 5 sunny lines covering all points in columns 2-5. The remaining column a=1 has points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5). These are covered by a vertical line x=1 (non-sunny). So total lines = 5 sunny + 1 vertical = 6 lines. But n=5, we need exactly n lines = 5 lines. Wait, we have n=5, but we used 5 sunny lines + 1 vertical = 6 lines, which is too many. Actually we need total lines = n = 5. So we cannot have 5 sunny lines plus any vertical lines; we must have exactly 5 lines total. So if we use 5 sunny lines, we cannot have any vertical lines. But we need to cover column a=1 points. However, maybe the sunny lines also cover column a=1 points. Let's check if any of the sunny lines we constructed also pass through points in column a=1. Let's see:\n\n- Line through (2,1) and (3,2) (slope 1) passes through (1,0) (since line equation y-1 = 1*(x-2) => y = x-1). At x=1, y=0, not a point (1,0) not in set (b must be >=1). So does not cover column a=1 points.\n\n- Line through (2,2) and (5,1) (slope -1/3) equation: y-2 = -1/3*(x-2) => y = -1/3*x + 2 + 2/3 = -1/3*x + 8/3. At x=1, y = -1/3 + 8/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer.\n\n- Line through (2,3) and (4,2) (slope -0.5) equation: y-3 = -0.5*(x-2) => y = -0.5*x + 4. At x=1, y = -0.5 + 4 = 3.5, not integer.\n\n- Line through (2,4) and (3,1) (slope -3) equation: y-4 = -3*(x-2) => y = -3*x + 10. At x=1, y = -3 + 10 = 7, not in set (b must be ≤ n+1-a = 5+1-1 = 5). So not needed.\n\n- Line through (4,1) and (3,3) (slope -2) equation: y-1 = -2*(x-4) => y = -2*x + 9. At x=1, y = -2 + 9 = 7, not in set.\n\nThus none of the sunny lines cover column a=1 points. So we need a vertical line x=1 to cover those points. That would be an extra line, making total lines = 6 > n. So k=5 is not possible for n=5 because we exceed the line count.\n\nBut maybe we can adjust the construction to use fewer vertical lines. For n=5, we have total lines = 5. If we use k=5 sunny lines, we have no vertical lines. So we need to cover all points with only sunny lines. Is that possible? Let's see if we can cover all point